p.src:
The same result is visible by writing the four-dimensional measure radially. Up to an angular constant, $\int_{\delta}\frac{d^4\Delta}{|\Delta|^6}
\sim
\int_{\delta}\frac{R^3\,dR}{R^6}
=\int_{\delta}\frac{dR}{R^3}
\sim\frac{1}{\delta^2}.$ This is why the large-distance upper limit is unimportant for the ultraviolet estimate: the integral is dominated by its lower endpoint.

