Again the radial form makes the distinction transparent: $\int_{\delta}^{R_{\mathrm{IR}}}
\frac{d^4\Delta}{|\Delta|^4}
\sim
\int_{\delta}^{R_{\mathrm{IR}}}\frac{R^3\,dR}{R^4}
=\log\!\left(\frac{R_{\mathrm{IR}}}{\delta}\right).$ Equal powers in measure and denominator produce a logarithm. More powers in the denominator produce an inverse power of the cutoff. The lecture is not asking us to evaluate angular factors; it is teaching us to recognize the ultraviolet answer on sight.

